Q: How to get Monday and Friday&#39;s date in C++ with date.h A: <p>To get the dates of Monday and Friday, you can do it like so by adapting the logic given by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/56507646/7277716">Howard Hinnant's answer here</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto todayDate = date::floor&lt;date::days&gt;(std::chrono::system_clock::now());
std::cout &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, todayDate) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

auto mondayDate = todayDate - (date::weekday{ todayDate } - date::Monday);
std::cout &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, mondayDate) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

auto fridayDate = mondayDate + (mondayDate - date::Thursday);
std::cout &lt;&lt; date::format(&quot;%F&quot;, fridayDate) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>If you need the Unix timestamp version:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto mondayTimestamp = mondayDate.time_since_epoch();
auto mondayDateSeconds = std::chrono::duration_cast&lt;std::chrono::seconds&gt;(mondayTimestamp).count();

auto fridayTimestamp = fridayDate.time_since_epoch();
auto fridaySeconds = std::chrono::duration_cast&lt;std::chrono::seconds&gt;(fridayTimestamp).count();
</code></pre>
<p>This should also work for the new C++20 <code>&lt;chrono&gt;</code> library as essentially it's Howard's library underneath</p>

Q: How to add animation to input placeholder A: <p>I guess you want to animate the text. I tried something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>input::placeholder {
  transform: translateY(-50%);
}

input:hover::placeholder {
  transform: translateY(0%);
}
</code></pre>
<p>It works also with color and other properties.</p>

Q: Fixing form input visibility and contrast issues between light and dark mode on mobile devices A: <pre><code>@media (max-width:768px){

/* ---------- LIGHT MODE ---------- */
body:not(.lecf-dark) .lecf-field input,
body:not(.lecf-dark) .lecf-field select,
body:not(.lecf-dark) .lecf-field textarea{

    background:#f8fafc;
    border:1.5px solid #bfc9d7;
    color:#1e293b;
    box-shadow:inset 0 1px 2px rgba(15,23,42,.03);
}

body:not(.lecf-dark) .lecf-field input::placeholder,
body:not(.lecf-dark) .lecf-field textarea::placeholder{
    color:#94a3b8;
    opacity:1;
}

body:not(.lecf-dark) .lecf-field label{
    color:#334155;
}


/* ---------- DARK MODE ---------- */
.lecf-dark .lecf-field input,
.lecf-dark .lecf-field select,
.lecf-dark .lecf-field textarea{

    background:rgba(255,255,255,0.04);
    border:1.5px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.10);
    color:#fff;
    box-shadow:inset 0 1px 2px rgba(255,255,255,.02);
}

.lecf-dark .lecf-field input::placeholder,
.lecf-dark .lecf-field textarea::placeholder{
    color:rgba(255,255,255,.45);
    opacity:1;
}

.lecf-dark .lecf-field label{
    color:rgba(255,255,255,.82);
}

}
</code></pre>
<p>Hope this helps, just explicitly defined the colors</p>
